engine: play GoldSrc demos

A GoldSrc demo (magic "HLDEMO") — what Half-Life and Counter-Strike have
recorded since 1998 — opens with playdemo like our own. Its network-message
frame is the same seven netchan sequence numbers and the same stripped server
message as a dem_read frame, behind 436 bytes of the client's own state, of
which the usercmd and the view angles are kept and run through prediction as
a dem_usercmd would be. Its client-side frames go to the calls that produced
them: events to CL_QueueEvent, weapon animations to CL_WeaponAnim, sounds to
S_StartSound, the client dll's buffer to Demo_ReadBuffer.

Demos from before October 2008 carry net protocol 46 or 47, and their
messages differ from 48 in three places (compLexity Demo Player, which
converted them for a decade, is the reference): svc_serverinfo's version
number, and its 21 bytes after a set VAC flag; protocol 46's five-bit weapon
index in svc_clientdata; protocol 46's svc_voiceinit without a quality byte.
CL_DemoGoldSrcProtocol tells the parser which it is reading.

Two things the engine did to any demo of a single-player game, found with a
2004 Half-Life recording: CL_ClearState shuts the console when serverinfo
arrives and only a multiplayer game reopens it, so the recording played out
in silence, its errors included; and the map checksum for a single-player
game is a constant, so the demo's real checksum never matched and the
client disconnected — behind that closed console. A recording now keeps
its console, gets the file's real checksum, and a mismatch is a warning:
the map we have may be a later edit of the one it was recorded on.

